From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 01:21:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] replay: a snapshot load judges its virtual timers at the
 loaded clock

Under record/replay, util/qemu-timer.c turns a virtual timer that is armed
already expired into vCPU work (timer_fire, no log event).  Loading a
snapshot re-arms device timers while QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL still runs at
the loading process's time: on x86 the APIC (MIG_PRI_APIC) loads before
the "timer" section that restores the clock.  x-rr-begin-record reloads its
snapshot at the snapshot's own time, so an APIC timer that was already due
when the VM stopped became vCPU work; the replaying process loads it from a
clock near 0, so the same timer went into the list, expired.  The replay
then needs a CHECKPOINT_CLOCK_VIRTUAL the log never has: the vCPU sees a
deadline of 0 on every round (icount_handle_deadline -> qemu_clock_notify ->
cpu_exit), breaks out before running its first instruction, and the replay
spins at icount 0 until killed (the x86 "replay hang": whenever the guest
was stopped with its APIC timer overdue).

replay_load_snapshot() brackets every load under record/replay with
timer_replay_load_begin/end: x-rr-begin-record and x-rr-begin-replay, the
-icount rrsnapshot= startup load and the reverse-debugging seek.  During
the load virtual timers are only armed and remembered, in arming order; at
the end the decision is made against the loaded clock, the way the
recorder saw it:
 - x-rr-begin-record's start snapshot (the recorder reloads it and records
   from the loaded state): each timer due at the loaded time becomes vCPU
   work.  The recorder's clock is already the loaded one, so it does what
   it did before (recordings made without this replay with it); the replay
   now matches it.
 - any other snapshot was taken by a run that went on from the saved
   state: its timers stay armed and fire through the log's checkpoints.
   (A seek used to load at the current replay clock, past the snapshot's,
   and turned timers due in between into vCPU work that forward replay ran
   through a checkpoint.)
Only main_loop_tlg's virtual list is deferred (device timers, armed under
the BQL, which the load holds), a failed load converts nothing, and a timer
that already ran (expire_time -1) is not fired again.

Any other disagreement between the log and the timers would still spin
silently, so:
 - at the end of a load, an expired virtual timer that the load did not
   arm is reported (warn_report): record and replay may disagree on it;
 - in replay, when the vCPU loop has run the virtual timers and one is
   still due at the same instruction count for 1000 rounds and 5 seconds,
   the expired timers (callback, opaque, expire time) are reported and the
   replay stops with replay_sync_error() instead of spinning.  This costs
   one replay_mode test when the virtual deadline is 0, nothing else when
   record/replay is off.
---
